00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.970 align:start position:0%
the Great Pacific Garbage Patch is one  of the most infamous examples of marine  plastic pollution but it's not actually  a vast island of floating trash  despite its expansive size in places it  can resemble a cloudy soup due to the  presence of micro plastics micro  plastics come in many shapes and forms  fibers from textiles and even cigarette  filters tiny fragments broken off larger  pieces of plastic and microbeads  like those used in toothpastes or face  washes which are small by design of the  10 million tonnes of plastic that enter  the ocean every year only about one  percent floats on the sea surface most  plastic ends up in the deep sea  including micro plastics new research  published in science uncovers a  previously unobserved setting in which  these miniscule pieces of plastic can  accumulate and how these hotspots form  to evaluate micro plastic abundance  researchers examined sediment from a  variety of seafloor features in the  tyranny and sea off the coast of Italy  from the continental shelf and slope  which is closer to the shore to deeper  parts of the water down to 900 meters in  depth one spoonful of sediment contained  a hundred ninety total pieces of plastic  meaning a square metre in the region  could contain upwards of 1.9 million  fragments and fibers to date this is the  highest concentration ever reported for  the seafloor this sample containing 190  pieces was taken from a contour a drift  an area adjacent to strong bottom  currents were gentle slopes of sediment  pile up lower concentrations were found  closer to the shore on the continental  slope and Shelf likely because the  current was too strong for deposits to  form this research demonstrates how deep  ocean currents influence where  microplastics end up similar to how  surface currents play a role in where  garbage patches form these same currents  also circulate vital oxygen and  nutrients to the seafloor  meaning that micro plastic hotspots  could coincide with extremely bio  diverse areas in the future researchers  hope to better understand the source of  these micro plastics by closely  examining their chemical structure  chemists could link plastic pieces to  the industries that created them areas  with other features like submarine fans  and canyons are ripe for the next round  of micro plastic studies as higher  resolution maps of the seafloor emerge  researchers will gain better insight  about where to locate more of the  missing 99% of deep-sea plastic  [Music]
 

